          "type": "input",
          "title": "You'll want to know: quitte à",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "quitte à — \"even if it means\"."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Je pars tôt, quitte à attendre une heure sur le quai. I am leaving early, even if it means waiting an hour on the platform."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "infinitive: you accept the cost. Être quitte — to be even, owe nothing."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "etymology",
          "title": "The word, taken apart",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "quitte comes from Old French quitte \"free, released\", taken from Latin quiētus \"at rest\" (EtymDB), from quiēs \"rest\" (Lewis & Short); a Medieval Latin form quittus is usually placed in between."
            }
          ]
        },
